          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for treating an acute migraine, and when is prevention considered?",
          "answer": "For treating an acute migraine, guidance suggests using oral treatments; however, non-oral methods may be necessary if a person cannot take medications orally due to vomiting or other reasons. It is typically recommended to try a treatment on two or three separate migraine attacks before concluding its ineffectiveness, as response can vary from attack to attack (source: National Headache Society guidance).\n\nPreventive treatment for migraines is considered when attacks are frequent or significantly disabling. Specifically, this is when a person experiences four or more migraine days per month with some associated disability. Preventive measures might also be appropriate for fewer attacks if they are severe, if acute treatments are ineffective, or if the individual prefers preventive therapy. The goal of preventive treatment is to reduce the frequency and severity of migraines, aiming for at least a 50% reduction in migraine days as a successful treatment response (source: National Headache Society guidance).\n\nI could not provide specific medications or detailed treatment protocols, as those are outside the scope of my guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
